&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;About the role&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We are seeking a Senior Harness Engineer to own the wire harnesses on our on-road autonomous vehicles, from design through fabrication and quality. In this role, you will take a harness from electrical architecture and schematic, to a released drawing package, to a built, tested, and installed assembly, working out how wire, connectors, routing, and protection come together on the vehicle. You will do this across our vehicle platforms and hardware revisions, and you will own the fabrication and inspection process every harness passes before it goes on a vehicle. This is a hands-on role based in the garage, on the build floor, and at our harness manufacturing partners.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What you’ll do&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Design the wire harnesses that connect sensors, compute, power distribution, and vehicle interfaces on our on-road vehicles: circuit allocation, wire and terminal selection, connectors and seals, splices, circuit protection, grounding, and shielding.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Produce and release the complete harness package: schematics, harness and formboard drawings, pinouts, wire lists, BOMs, and manufacturing drawings, with 3D routing and length verification against the vehicle model.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Define routing, retention, protection, and service loops on the vehicle, and verify fit, bend radius, clearance, and strain relief on the first installation of each new design.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Build and test prototype harnesses in house, and run first article builds and production releases with harness manufacturing partners, including DFM and DFA review, tooling and formboard requirements, and lead time planning.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Write the work instructions, inspection criteria, and end-of-line test procedures for harness fabrication, and keep them current through engineering change.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Hold harness workmanship to IPC/WHMA-A-620: crimp validation by pull test and crimp height, incoming inspection of purchased harnesses, and continuity and hipot testing before a harness is released to the build floor.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Manage harness variants and configuration across vehicle platforms and hardware revisions, and run engineering changes through PLM with effectivity and cut-in defined for vehicles in build and in the fleet.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Own harness issues from the build floor and the fleet: reproduce the failure, establish root cause with technicians and integration engineers, and drive corrective action with design or the manufacturing partner through verification on the builds that follow.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Qualify harness components and assemblies for the vehicle environment, including connector sealing, temperature, vibration, and chemical exposure, and turn the results into design rules and an approved component list.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Support vehicle builds and bring-up in the garage, including harness installation, debug of power and network faults, and rework and repair decisions.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Standardize connectors, terminals, and harness components with electrical, mechanical, and supply chain, covering cost, availability, and serviceability. Internal customers are vehicle integration, manufacturing, and fleet operations.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What we’re looking for&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Bachelor&#39;s degree in Electrical, Mechanical, Mechatronics, or Aerospace Engineering, or a related field. Equivalent hands-on experience will be considered in place of the degree.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;5+ years designing and releasing wire harnesses for vehicles, aircraft, or robotic systems, in automotive, autonomous vehicle, aerospace, or commercial vehicle programs, including harnesses you took from concept through production release.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Experience producing complete, buildable harness documentation: schematics, harness and formboard drawings, pinouts, wire lists, and BOMs, in an electrical design tool such as Capital or Zuken E3.series, with 3D routing in CATIA, NX, SolidWorks, or equivalent.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Working knowledge of wire, terminal, connector, seal, and circuit protection selection, grounding and shielding practice, and the sealed automotive connector systems in common use.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Hands-on harness fabrication: cutting, stripping, crimping, soldering, splicing, shielding, loom and overbraid, labeling, and continuity testing, with the ability to build and rework a harness yourself.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Working knowledge of IPC/WHMA-A-620 and crimp validation methods, including pull testing, crimp height measurement, and cross-sectioning.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Experience working with a contract harness manufacturer on DFM, first articles, quality issues, and engineering changes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Vehicle network and interface fundamentals as they apply to harness design: CAN and CAN FD, LIN, Automotive Ethernet, and high-speed sensor links such as GMSL or FPD-Link, including twisted pair, shield, and termination requirements.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Experience with a PLM or PDM system and an engineering change process, including effectivity and cut-in on hardware already in the field.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Working knowledge of structured problem solving on build and field failures: 8D, 5 Whys, fishbone, and FMEA.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Clear written communication for drawings, work instructions, failure reports, and supplier correspondence.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ability to work onsite five days a week in San Francisco, with regular time in the garage and on the build floor, and travel to harness manufacturing partners and test sites. Valid driver&#39;s license with a clean driving record. The role requires lifting up to 50 lbs and working at roof level and under vehicles, with or without reasonable accommodation.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
